        "market_heading": "Why home staging matters in the Vancouver market",
        "market_text": "<p>Vancouver buyers shop online first, and the region's housing stock is hard on empty photos: glass condo towers shot against grey skies read cold, and compact downtown floor plans look even smaller unfurnished. Home staging fills the square footage believably, so a 550 sq ft Yaletown one-bed reads as a home rather than a box with a view.</p><p>The light is a genuine technical problem here for much of the year. An empty white-walled unit photographed under overcast Vancouver skies has almost no warmth in it, and no amount of exposure correction adds the furniture, texture and colour that make a room feel lived in.</p><p>Vancouver is also where Bella actually is. The designers staging listings across North America work from here, which means local agents are dealing with a studio in their own time zone rather than an overseas production line — and the work is composed by hand rather than generated.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Staged for the Vancouver buyer",
        "local_text": "<p>Designers stage clean contemporary for Yaletown, Coal Harbour and downtown towers, West Coast warmth for Kitsilano, Mount Pleasant and East Van character homes, and family looks for Burnaby, Richmond and the North Shore.</p><p>Compact floor plans get furnished to scale rather than flattered. In a market this expensive per square foot, buyers measure carefully — staging that oversells a room loses the trust it was meant to build.</p>",
